{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n     6\t\n     7\t## Core Philosophy\n     8\t\n     9\tEverything is data.\n    10\tInterpretation > abstraction.\n    11\tExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n    19\t   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n    20\t   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n    21\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    22\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    23\t\n    24\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    25\t\n    26\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    27\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    28\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n    29\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    30\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    31\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    32\t- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n    33\t\n    34\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    35\t\n    36\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    37\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n    38\tSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n    39\t\n    40\tSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n    41\t\n    42\t## Streams\n    43\t\n    44\tAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n    45\tNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n    46\tSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\n    47\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n    48\tStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n    49\t\n    50\tA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n    51\tof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\n    52\tsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\n    53\tstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\n    54\tthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\n    55\tanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\n    56\texists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\n    57\tthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\n    58\tand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\n    59\tbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\n    60\tthe stream's own.\n    61\t\n    62\tFunctions can take anything and return anything.\n    63\tAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n    64\t\n    65\tSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\n    66\tSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n    67\t\n    68\t### Host Boundaries\n    69\t\n    70\tA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n    71\t[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n    72\t\n    73\t## Architectural Commitments\n    74\t\n    75\t- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n    76\t- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n    77\t- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n    78\t- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n    79\t\n    80\t## Design Principles\n    81\t\n    82\tCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\n    83\tStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n    84\t\n    85\t**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\n    86\tcomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\n    87\twithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n    88\t\n    89\t- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n    90\t  ordinary streams.\n    91\t- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n    92\t  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n    93\t- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n    94\t- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n    95\t  auto-index observer.\n    96\t- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n    97\t  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n    98\t\n    99\t**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\n   100\tcanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\n   101\tshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n   102\t`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\n   103\tquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\n   104\tconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\n   105\tretired `:global` tag in\n   106\t[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\n   107\tcase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n   108\t\n   109\tSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\n   110\tSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n   111\t\n   112\t## Code Style\n   113\t\n   114\t- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n   115\t- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n   116\t- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n   117\t- Avoid cleverness.\n   118\t- Name things after what they do to streams.\n   119\t\n   120\t## Development Workflow\n   121\t\n   122\t- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n   123\t- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n   124\t- Do not optimize prematurely.\n   125\t- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n   126\t- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n   127\t- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n   128\t  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n   129\t  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n   130\t  leaves to review.\n   131\t\n   132\t### Problem Decomposition\n   133\t\n   134\tIf a problem appears complex:\n   135\t1. Decompose it into streams.\n   136\t2. Identify invariants.\n   137\t3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n   138\t4. Make causality explicit.\n   139\t5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n   140\t\n   141\t## Operational & Specialized Guides\n   142\t\n   143\t- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n   144\t- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n   145\t- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n   146\t- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n   147\t- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/dao.star.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# dao.star: Hypermedia Web Applications in datom.world\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design. Mode 1 is specified and buildable on\n     4\t[`dao.stream.http.md`](./dao.stream.http.md) and [`dao.lease.md`](./dao.lease.md).\n     5\tMode 2 is proposed and waits on fenced custody, version 1 of\n     6\t[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md)\n     7\t(owner ruling 2026-10-07). The published framing is\n     8\t[`daostar-vs-datastar-vs-electric-clojure.blog`](../../public/chp/blog/daostar-vs-datastar-vs-electric-clojure.blog).\n     9\t\n    10\t## Core Philosophy\n    11\t\n    12\t`dao.star` is not a library or an adapter; it is the architectural pattern for building web applications natively in `datom.world`. It emerges from the intersection of two aligned philosophies:\n    13\t1. **datom.world**: Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data flowing over explicit, append-only streams (Axioms 1 and 2). It avoids compiler magic, instead exposing the network explicitly via data.\n    14\t2. **Datastar**: The browser should be a dumb, stateless interpreter of hypermedia (HTML) delivered over Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n    15\t\n    16\tDatastar interprets the SSE projection of an egress `dao.stream`. That is enough to eliminate frontend state management, reactive DAG compilers (like Electric Clojure), and proprietary RPC adapters: standard HTTP in, standard SSE out, and every piece in between is an ordinary stream or an ordinary interpreter.\n    17\t\n    18\t`dao.star` provides two operational modes under one programming model: **The Thin Client** (Arc-style sequential UI) and **The Fat Client** (mobile continuations).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Roles, not nodes.** \"Server\" below means the peer whose composition holds the HTTP boundary; \"browser\" means a peer that can only dial. Neither is a kind of node, and no protocol privilege attaches to either (`datom.world.md`, Design Principles). The HTTP boundary's two media are ordinary streams, so the agent answering requests may run on a peer other than the one that listens, over `dao.stream.remote.md` reflections.\n    21\t\n    22\t---\n    23\t\n    24\t## Mode 1: The Thin Client (Arc-Style Sequential UI)\n    25\t\n    26\tIn the thin client model the browser holds zero business logic and zero shared mutable state. Datastar acts as a dumb terminal rendering HTML patches, while `yin.vm` executes linear, sequential code on the server.\n    27\t\n    28\t### Flow\n    29\t\n    30\t1. **Events In**: A user clicks a button (`data-on-click=\"@post('/k/<token>')\"`). Datastar fires a standard HTTP request. The generic HTTP boundary (`dao.stream.http.md`) mints an exchange id, deposits the request as an unjudged `:http/request` event into the ingress `dao.stream` the composition wired, and holds the socket open. It reads nothing inside the request.\n    31\t2. **Routing**: A router step in the `dao.star` composition reads the ingress with its own cursor, classifies each request by its path (*Sessions and request kinds*), and forwards it to the medium of the session its token names, together with every later terminal event of that exchange. The boundary does not route.\n    32\t3. **Resuming**: The session's driver, on its next poll, observes the input at its own cursor and steps the parked continuation. Nothing wakes anything; a stream invokes nothing.\n    33\t4. **Semantics and State**: The `yin.vm` agent evaluates business logic linearly. The UI is a derivation of the continuation's state, never stored: the agent projects it into Datastar's SSE wire format (`event: datastar-patch-elements\\ndata: <div>...</div>`) through one pure projection function the composition supplies.\n    34\t5. **Effects Out**: The agent appends to the egress `dao.stream`, tagged with the exchange id: an `:http/head` effect (status, `Content-Type: text/event-stream`), one `:http/chunk` per SSE event with the fully constructed string, and `:http/end` when the response is complete. A subscription exchange simply never ends.\n    35\t6. **Dumb Socket Routing**: The boundary's `egress-step` observes the egress stream, matches the exchange id to the open socket, and flushes the unmodified payload. Datastar morphs the DOM. The agent parks again, waiting for the next input, for as long as it takes.\n    36\t\n    37\t### Sessions and request kinds\n    38\t\n    39\tA **lineage** is one session's chain of occurrences: a root and every successor a consumed input produced. **The token is the occurrence id**, minted unguessable by the driver; the lineage's identity is its root's id. Every projected UI carries the id of the occurrence that rendered it. The lineage's **current** occurrence is the one with no successor.\n    40\t\n    41\tThe driver appends one fact per transition to the **session-facts** medium, and appends it with `:dao.stream/ok` before any UI carrying the new id is projected:\n    42\t\n    43\t```clojure\n    44\t{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/occurrence S}                            ; root\n    45\t{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/occurrence O2 :dao.star/predecessor O1}  ; consumption\n    46\t{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/ended :halted}                           ; the program finished\n    47\t```\n    48\t\n    49\tThe router materializes occurrence → lineage → current from these facts and from the lease medium's `:lapsed` records (*Liveness*), as `dao.space.index` materializes a covered index, never from a table it keeps. The session-facts medium is a complete-history transport (`dao.stream.md`, Complete history); its growth is bounded only by OD-4's resolution, an open dependency.\n    50\t\n    51\t**Observation order.** A fact's `ok` is publication, not the router's observation of it, so the router never classifies from a projection that may lag. Each router pass reads the ingress first, then drains its session-facts and lease-medium cursors to `blocked`, then classifies what it read. A token reaches the ingress only through a UI projected after its fact was accepted, so a fact precedes every request that names it, and a pass that has read the request has the fact to drain. The same holds for successor ids. Over reflections, acceptance is the source's `ok` on the link's event writer, not the outbound `ok` (`dao.stream.remote.md`, 2.4), and the driver projects only after it.\n    52\t\n    53\tThe router classifies by path; the boundary reads neither path nor token.\n    54\t\n    55\t| Path | Kind | What it does |\n    56\t|---|---|---|\n    57\t| `/` (no token) | begin | The root program starts a fresh lineage and answers a complete `text/html` document: `datastar.js`, actions carrying the root id, and `data-init=\"@get('/s/<token>')\"`. |\n    58\t| `/s/<token>` | subscribe | Opens the session's long-lived SSE exchange. Consumes nothing and advances nothing: the driver answers the head and a projection of the current occurrence, and every later patch goes here too. Reconnecting with the original URL is a new subscribe, resolved to the current occurrence however many inputs have passed. |\n    59\t| `/k/<token>`, token current | input | Consumed by the current occurrence (*Inputs, order and replay*). |\n    60\t| `/k/<token>`, token consumed | stale | Runs nothing. Answered by a projection of the current occurrence, then `:http/end`. |\n    61\t| any token of an ended or unknown lineage | begin | Halted, lapsed, or lost to a restart: answered as `/`. Unknown is judged only after the drain above. |\n    62\t\n    63\tSubscribe and stale requests are **re-projections**: the driver applies the projection function to the parked state without stepping it.\n    64\t\n    65\t### Signals\n    66\t\n    67\tA Datastar request carries the client's signals: in the `datastar` query parameter for GET and DELETE, in the JSON body otherwise. The boundary deposits them where HTTP put them, inside `:http/query` or `:http/body`, unread.\n    68\t\n    69\tDecoding is interpretation. `dao.star` supplies one pure projection from a request event to a signals map. The ingress tuple gains no slot for it, and signals become datoms only when an application interpreter derives them from the retained ingress stream (`datom.world.md`, Derive, don't persist).\n    70\t\n    71\tSignals are client-held state that moves, not shared state: each request carries an immutable snapshot, and the agent changes them only by appending a `datastar-patch-signals` effect. They are also the client's unverified claim. A signal is input to a continuation, never its state and never authority.\n    72\t\n    73\t### Inputs, order and replay\n    74\t\n    75\t**The token is the gate.** It is the request's only routing and idempotence key, and until ShiBi is load-bearing it is the sole gate on Mode 1 input: a pre-shared value in `dao.shibi.md`'s sense (owner ruling 2026-10-07).\n    76\t\n    77\t**Order is the ingress position.** Two inputs for one occurrence are not concurrent once deposited: one is earlier. Custody decides who consumes it. In a one-process composition the session's single driver is the only resumer; that exclusion is process-scoped and says so. Where more than one peer observes the ingress, resumption is a take, and a take is a lease on the occurrence (UCF §7.7).\n    78\t\n    79\t**One input, one successor.** Consuming an input advances occurrence `O` to its successor `O'` and appends the lineage fact. The reply goes to the exchange that carried the consumed input; patches for the session go to its open subscription exchanges, which the driver holds as its own interpreter state, built from the subscribe requests and terminal events routed to it.\n    80\t\n    81\t**Stale input is one-shot** (owner ruling 2026-10-07). A double click, a retry, a replayed URL, a second tab or the back button names an `O` that already has a successor. It runs nothing and is re-projected. The UI is a derivation, so answering twice is harmless, and a retry whose first reply was lost still converges. Mode 1 continuations are one-shot; multi-shot forks are deferred.\n    82\t\n    83\t**Effects.** Exactly-once needs a fenced writer into an enrolled consumer (UCF §7.7.5). Every other stream is at-least-once and declared so.\n    84\t\n    85\t### Liveness\n    86\t\n    87\tTwo leased resources, kept apart. Neither adds a clock to the wait for input: a parked continuation's `next` on its input is `blocked` until a request arrives, on a Tuesday or a Thursday.\n    88\t\n    89\t**The open exchange is leased.** The judge runs inside the peer that holds the HTTP boundary and is composed with the boundary's `close-exchange` as reclaim procedure and `open-exchanges` as its inventory (`dao.stream.http.md`, Inventory). For each `:http/request` it observes it grants unsolicited on a lease medium it writes: subject `{:http/exchange x}`, holder the egress medium's identity, a duration in `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`. Reclaim closes the exchange; the adapter deposits `:http/closed`, then the judge records `:lapsed`. A responder that keeps an exchange open observes its grant and appends `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal :dao.lease/lease L}` to the egress medium at under half the duration, from its own control flow, against its own tick stream. The judge and `egress-step` are peer observers of that medium; each ignores the other's facts. A one-shot responder answers inside the granted duration and renews nothing. An exchange nobody answers lapses by `:silence`; Datastar's reconnect opens a new one.\n    90\t\n    91\t**A gap is loss, and nothing is forwarded about it.** The judge, on an ingress `gap`, reconciles both ways against the inventory: an open exchange without a lease is granted afresh, and a leased exchange absent from the inventory is recorded `:lapsed` with cause `:policy`, so nothing is held unleased and nothing closed stays leased (inventory reconciliation; `dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant is reserved for a lost ledger). The router forwards nothing about a gap: a request or terminal event lost in the evicted window is unrouted, and a forward whose `append!` answers anything but `ok` is the same loss; an unrouted request's exchange lapses by `:silence`. Drivers learn that an exchange is gone from the lease medium they already read for their grants: a `:lapsed` record for one of their exchanges drops it (`dao.lease.md`, Carriage). A driver whose cursor on any medium it reads answers `gap` forgets every subscription it holds and renews none; they lapse by `:silence` and close, and Datastar's reconnect subscribes again. Session media are declared at assembly like any deposit destination, evict-oldest with a capacity, and sized against the driver's lag.\n    92\t\n    93\t**Keep-alive is the responder's.** On its tick it may append an `:http/chunk` carrying an SSE comment line. That chunk is bytes for the wire, so the host discovers a dead peer by writing; the renewal is evidence for the judge. Neither stands in for the other. A peer's close or a failed write is deposited as `:http/closed`; the responder reads it as data and drops the exchange, and the judge ends the exchange lease with cause `:policy`.\n    94\t\n    95\t**The lineage is leased** (owner ruling 2026-10-07; the holder choice below is the architect's). A lineage nobody returns to is reclaimed through `dao.lease.md`, not by a timer. The peer holding the parked record is the grantor. When a lineage begins, its judge grants unsolicited on the lease medium: subject and holder `{:dao.star/lineage S}`, duration the composition's idle allowance, `:dao.lease/max` optional. Attribution is by envelope key: the session-facts medium is written only by session drivers, each stamping `:dao.star/lineage` on its facts, renewals and release, and the judge's resolver reads that key; until ShiBi, any writer of that medium can renew, the same exposure the exchange lease has. The driver observes its grant before consuming or projecting, renews at under half the duration from its own control flow, on each consumed input and on its tick while it holds an open subscription, and **stops at its bound**: past it, it consumes nothing and projects nothing, and an input arriving then is handed to the root program as begin. So a connected session is retained for as long as it stays connected, by design, bounded only by the cap; a disconnected one lapses after the idle allowance. The lease ends with the lineage: released by the driver on halt, lapsed by the judge on silence. Reclaim retires the runnable resource, the current parked record; the `:lapsed` record on the lease medium is the judge's own and is what ends the lineage for the router. History, successor facts and lease records are kept (`dao.lease.md`: a reclaim frees the resource, never the record). The subject is distinct from Mode 2's custody subject `{:yin.k/occurrence O}` on the arbitration medium. Leases are process-scoped until the composition settles the three durable duties `dao.lease.md` names.\n    96\t\n    97\tSizing: duration exceeds twice the renewal interval; the keep-alive interval stays under the shortest proxy or NAT idle timeout on the path; the ingress window exceeds router and judge lag and each session medium's window exceeds its driver's; a boundary restart loses sockets and ledger together, so its exchange inventory to reclaim is empty. A false lapse is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n    98\t\n    99\t---\n   100\t\n   101\t## Mode 2: The Fat Client (Mobile Continuations via UCF)\n   102\t\n   103\tStatus: proposed. Everything in this section waits on UCF version 1, fenced custody with a `dao.space` transactor as arbitration authority (`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` §7.7, 7.7.8). Version 0 is fork-only and cannot offer the exclusivity below.\n   104\t\n   105\tWhile `dao.star` champions the dumb terminal, it is not locked into it. Because `yin.vm` continuations are pure data, they can cross the network boundary to act as SPA-style fat clients for low-latency local interaction.\n   106\t\n   107\t### Flow\n   108\t\n   109\t1. **Exporting**: A `yin.vm` continuation on the server parks and enters the exporting state (UCF §7.7.4): its wait set is detached, so no poll or local resume can perform an effect while the value is in other hands. The lift produces a UCF handoff body, canonical and content-addressed, publishes it (*Handoff carrier*), and the driver records the offer on the arbitration medium.\n   110\t2. **Handoff**: The SSE patch carries the notice, not the body. A browser-side adapter deposits the notice on a browser-local stream that the browser's `yin.vm` reads; Datastar hands nothing to anyone.\n   111\t3. **Custody**: The browser `yin.vm` fetches the body by address over its WebSocket channel, proposes on the arbitration medium, and resumes only on observing its grant. Custody is exclusive because the lease is: the state moves to the browser rather than being shared with it, and the server's copy stays fenced.\n   112\t4. **Transparent Remote Access**: A continuation that needs a server-held stream uses the portable descriptors it carried and `attach!`es a reflection over WebSockets. Descriptors carry no authorization: the served entries are gated and leased for the migrated continuation (`dao.stream.remote.md`, sections 6 and 7). On channel loss a reflection answers `channel-gone` and reattachment is the continuation's own step on the same descriptor; a reclaimed entry answers `not-found` and is gone, not disconnected.\n   113\t5. **Migration Back**: The continuation re-serializes itself as a successor and offers it to the server, which admits it under *Admitting a continuation back*.\n   114\t\n   115\t### Handoff carrier\n   116\t\n   117\t**Architect ruling, not yet confirmed by the owner (2026-10-07).** The SSE patch carries a notice and the body travels by address over the browser's WebSocket channel. The notice is the body's content address, the arbitration descriptor, and a content coordinate `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` (`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`, section 1).\n   118\t\n   119\tThe path: before the notice is appended, the lift publishes the body to the server's content store and appends the address to the session-facts medium as the lineage's export. The `requests` and `answers` entries are a pair served per lineage, so answers are not readable by other lineages, and gated with the lineage token as the pre-ShiBi credential; the gate's `verify` admits a `get` only for an address the lineage's own export facts name. The browser's fetch is the stepped content client, clock-free; it verifies the body by re-hashing (UCF §7.8, lower step 2). Three absences are distinct and all arrive as data: missing content is the service's `:jing/found? false`, a missing service is the remote `not-found` error, and a disallowed address is `:dao.stream/refused` at the gate. In each the browser holds nothing, and the exporter, a candidate like any other, proposes for itself under its own policy (UCF §7.7.4), so an unfetched offer strands nothing. Confidentiality is the gate's and the pair's: an address is a name, not an authorization.\n   120\t\n   121\tReasons: the body is canonical CBOR and SSE is text, so inlining means a second encoding; the egress medium is shared, and a body on it discloses the store slice to every observer, where a notice discloses a name; large values travel as content addresses (`dao.stream.md`, Granularity); and the browser `yin.vm` needs the channel anyway for custody and remote access, so the carrier adds no path.\n   122\t\n   123\tRejected: the body base64-encoded inside a `datastar-patch-signals` or element attribute (double encoding, disclosure on egress, Datastar's payload size); a second HTTP GET by address (a second boundary path for what the channel already carries).\n   124\t\n   125\t### Admitting a continuation back\n   126\t\n   127\tA continuation returning from the browser is admitted as untrusted code and state. It ran where nothing can attest to it, so it is treated as the controlled mode of `dao.space.security.md` treats any pushed interpreter.\n   128\t\n   129\t1. **Custody first** (UCF §7.7, unchanged). The successor must name, in `:yin.k/origin`, the lease the ledger holds live for that holder, and arrive on the holder's own medium. Otherwise `:yin.k/not-holder`.\n   130\t2. **Validate as hostile bytes** (UCF §7.8, lower steps 1 to 4), under a size bound and a step budget the composition declares.\n   131\t3. **Rebind authority; never read it from the value.** Lower into an isolated store. Primitives and FFI ops resolve from the receiver's registry, restricted to the set the composition delegated to this lineage at export. A stream marker attaches only if its identity is in that delegated set, or its channel is the returning peer's own. Anything else is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it.\n   132\t4. **No check survives the trip.** A decision the program made before export may have been skipped by a forged frame. Whatever must hold after return is enforced by a gate on a stream, or stays home in a continuation that never left and answers the migrated one over streams.\n   133\t\n   134\tThe test: a returned continuation can do nothing its principal could not do directly through the gated streams it was given.\n   135\t\n   136\t**Export is disclosure.** The lifted store slice and values are readable by the browser and by every peer the content gate admits. The composition inspects the computed closure before publishing.\n   137\t\n   138\t**Until effect handlers honor a delegated set** (`dao.space.security.md`, Honest Limits), migration back is admitted from allow-listed peers only (owner ruling 2026-10-07). ShiBi later plugs in at the same gates.\n   139\t\n   140\t---\n   141\t\n   142\t## Design Invariants Maintained\n   143\t\n   144\t- **No Shared Mutable State**: The UI is a pure projection (Mode 1), or custody of the continuation is held under an exclusive lease (Mode 2). State is never shared across the boundary; it moves, and the mover is fenced.\n   145\t- **Multiplexed Correlation**: Connections are not mapped 1:1 to streams. The HTTP boundary observes a shared egress stream and routes by exchange id. The cost is shared: an egress `gap` closes every open exchange, and a composition under that pressure shards the egress.\n   146\t- **Host Isolation**: The HTTP boundary is a dumb pipe that knows nothing of Datastar or `yin.vm` syntax. Datastar is a dumb renderer that knows nothing of backend state. Under Mode 2 a `yin.vm` runs beside it, not inside it.\n   147\t- **Data > Macros**: Compile-time slicing (Electric Clojure) is rejected. Network boundaries are crossed dynamically at runtime via reified data on explicit streams.\n   148\t- **No callbacks, no implicit control flow**: every arrival is a deposit, every departure is an effect, and every wait is a `blocked` answer at a cursor.\n   149\t- **No hidden state**: a driver's open subscriptions and the router's token index are interpreter state derived from media each reads with its own cursor, rebuilt from those media or honestly lost on a gap.\n   150\t\n   151\t## Deferred\n   152\t\n   153\t- **Multi-shot forks** (Arc's back button resuming a sibling branch): branch identity and subscription routing with a non-unique current are unspecified.\n   154\t- **Local projection** in Mode 2: the browser `yin.vm` appends the same Datastar-format effects to a browser-local egress stream read by a browser-side boundary adapter; same vocabulary, different boundary.\n   155\t- **Compaction of the session-facts medium**: waits on `dao.stream.md` OD-4.\n   156\t- **The content service as rules**: `dao.jing.content` is a plan section, not a spec; the notice above depends on it.\n   157\t- **An HTTP channel for peers without WebSocket**: `dao.stream.http.md`, Deferred.\n   158\t\n   159\t## Completion Criteria\n   160\t\n   161\t1. **Thin Client**: A `yin.vm` agent receives a Datastar input through the HTTP boundary's ingress stream, answers with `:http/head`, SSE-formatted `:http/chunk`s and `:http/end` on the egress stream, parks, and resumes on the next input for its current token. Also: a request with no token begins a lineage; a subscribe consumes nothing and a re-subscribe after several inputs receives the current projection; a stale token is re-projected; a closed socket is observed as `:http/closed`; a silent exchange lapses and closes; a lineage nobody returns to lapses, its parked record is retired and its facts remain; a request naming a just-published token is never answered as begin; after an ingress gap no exchange is unleased, no closed exchange stays leased, and no driver renews one.\n   162\t2. **Fat Client Handoff**, conditional on UCF version 1 landing: a `yin.vm` agent exports under exclusive custody, the SSE patch carries the notice, a CLJS `yin.vm` in the browser fetches the body through the lineage's gated content pair, verifies it, obtains the grant, resumes, and reaches a server-held stream through a gated, leased reflection. Migration back from an allow-listed peer is admitted under *Admitting a continuation back*. The carrier shape is an architect ruling pending the owner's confirmation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1792406555890-architect-dao-star-round4-r2.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-19 10:46:00 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-10-19 17:46:00 +07\n     3\tCoding-Agent: claude\n     4\tSession-ID: 06daf2e5-59b2-4d67-9c9e-94995df5c027\n     5\t\n     6\tBoth defects verified against the text and fixed in `docs/design/dao.star.md` only (162 lines, +2 net); `dao.stream.http.md` is unchanged and still application-agnostic. EDN blocks parse (3 and 6 forms). Nothing staged. No owner decision touched.\n     7\t\n     8\t| # | Defect | Disposition |\n     9\t|---|---|---|\n    10\t| 1 | Token race: router classifies \"unknown\" from a projection that may lag the fact (lines 41–59) | **Fixed** by an observation-order rule; no new mechanism |\n    11\t| 2 | Unchecked gap delivery: forwarded gap event may never be observed; session-medium `gap` unhandled (line 89, criterion line 159) | **Fixed** by deleting the forwarded event; recovery rides the lease medium drivers already read |\n    12\t\n    13\t## Ruling 1: observation order, not acknowledgement\n    14\t\n    15\tThe race is real: `ok` on the session-facts append is publication, and the router's cursor is independent. The fix is a pass-order rule on the router, one paragraph: each pass reads the ingress first, then drains its session-facts and lease-medium cursors to `blocked`, then classifies. The argument that this closes the race is causal, not temporal: a token can reach the ingress only through a UI projected after its fact was accepted (the existing rule at line 41), so the fact's position precedes every request naming it, and a pass that has read the request has the fact available to drain. The same holds for successor ids, since consumption facts obey the same append-before-project rule. \"Unknown\" is now judged only after that drain (table row amended). Over reflections, acceptance means the source's `ok` on the link's event writer, not the outbound `ok` (`dao.stream.remote.md` 2.4); the driver projects only after it.\n    16\t\n    17\tRejected: a readiness acknowledgement stream before exposing the token (a wait, and a second medium for a fact the order of two reads already settles); draining facts *before* reading the ingress (the window between drain and ingress read is exactly the race); the router keeping a table of minted tokens (hidden state, and it would have to be fed by a call).\n    18\t\n    19\t## Ruling 2: no forwarded gap; loss is loss, and the lease medium carries recovery\n    20\t\n    21\tThe forwarded `{:dao.star/event :dao.star/gap}` was an unchecked append whose observation nothing guaranteed, and it is gone. What remains was already in the design and needed only to be completed:\n    22\t\n    23\t- The judge's inventory reconciliation now runs both ways: an open exchange without a lease is granted; a leased exchange absent from the inventory is recorded `:lapsed` with cause `:policy`. After an ingress gap nothing is unleased and nothing closed stays leased.\n    24\t- Drivers learn an exchange is gone from the lease medium they already read for their grants: a `:lapsed` for one of their exchanges drops it. This is `dao.lease.md` Carriage verbatim (a holder observes the grantor's record), not a new channel.\n    25\t- A driver whose cursor on *any* medium it reads answers `gap` forgets every subscription and renews none; they lapse by `:silence`, and Datastar re-subscribes.\n    26\t- The router's forward outcomes are checked and the policy is one sentence: anything but `ok` is loss of that event, same as a request lost in the evicted window; an unrouted request's exchange lapses by `:silence`. The router appends nothing else.\n    27\t- Session media are declared at assembly like any deposit destination (evict-oldest, a capacity) and sized against the driver's lag; added to Sizing.\n    28\t\n    29\tCriterion 1 now reads: a just-published token is never answered as begin; after an ingress gap no exchange is unleased, no closed exchange stays leased, and no driver renews one.\n    30\t\n    31\tRejected: ordered, acknowledged notification delivery to each driver (a request/response conversation between router and driver, which re-introduces the waiting the design removed); drivers holding their own ingress cursors (O(sessions × ingress)); drivers probing closed exchanges by writing (the boundary ignores effects for closed exchanges silently, by design in `dao.stream.http.md`).\n    32\t\n    33\t## Open dependencies, unchanged\n    34\t\n    35\t`dao.stream.md` OD-4 (bounds the complete-history session-facts medium); `dao.jing.content` as rules (Mode 2 notice); whether a served pair may be gated by a non-channel credential (`dao.shibi.md`, stub). Nothing new added to Deferred this round.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.http.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream HTTP Boundary\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\n     5\tdisagreement; sibling of [`dao.stream.ws.md`](./dao.stream.ws.md), whose\n     6\tdeposit model and admission rule it reuses. Every sentence below is a rule.\n     7\tThis document is application-agnostic: it specifies HTTP exchanges as\n     8\tevents and effects and nothing about what they carry.\n     9\t\n    10\t## What it is\n    11\t\n    12\tThe HTTP boundary is not a transport and owns no stream. It is an adapter\n    13\t(events in) and a host effect interpreter (effects out) over two ordinary\n    14\tmedia the composition supplies: an **ingress** medium it deposits into and\n    15\tan **egress** medium it reads. It has no reader surface, no `create!`, and\n    16\thands no handle upward. It never reads a request body, a query string or a\n    17\tresponse chunk. Because nothing host-local crosses either medium, both are\n    18\t`:portable-values`, and the interpreter answering requests may sit on any\n    19\tpeer (see *No privileged node*).\n    20\t\n    21\t## Exchange\n    22\t\n    23\tOne request and its response are one attachment; a connection is not\n    24\t(HTTP/1.1 keep-alive and HTTP/2 multiplexing are connection facts below\n    25\tthe transform). The boundary mints `:http/exchange` under the contract's\n    26\tserver-minted attachment rule (`dao.stream.md`, Envelopes): plain data,\n    27\tunique among the exchanges it can distinguish, and never reused for the\n    28\tlifetime of the egress medium. It carries the boundary's incarnation, so a\n    29\tstale effect retained from before a restart can never match a new socket.\n    30\t\n    31\t**Inventory.** The set of exchanges currently open is a resource the\n    32\tboundary possesses. It is not derivable from the ingress medium, which\n    33\tevicts. The boundary exposes it as one transport-owned operation beside\n    34\t`egress-step`, `open-exchanges`, answering the open ids, together with the\n    35\tidempotent `close-exchange`. Both exist for a `dao.lease.md` judge composed\n    36\tinside this boundary (reclaim procedure and restart inventory); code\n    37\twritten against the contract may not reach for them.\n    38\t\n    39\t## Events in\n    40\t\n    41\tWire framing, header parsing and size limits happen below the transform;\n    42\tan over-limit or malformed request is answered there (400, 413, 503) and is\n    43\tnot an event. Every valid request is deposited unjudged. In these examples\n    44\tthe exchange id is `[incarnation n]` and strings stand for byte values:\n    45\t\n    46\t```clojure\n    47\t{:http/exchange [7 42]\n    48\t :http/event    :http/request\n    49\t :http/method   :post\n    50\t :http/path     \"/action\"\n    51\t :http/query    \"a=1\"\n    52\t :http/headers  {\"accept\" \"text/event-stream\"}\n    53\t :http/body     \"{\\\"count\\\":1}\"}\n    54\t\n    55\t{:http/exchange [7 42] :http/event :http/ended}\n    56\t{:http/exchange [7 42] :http/event :http/closed}\n    57\t```\n    58\t\n    59\t`:http/method` is a lower-case keyword; `:http/query` and `:http/body` are\n    60\tabsent when the request has none; header names are lower-cased.\n    61\t\n    62\tExactly one terminal event is deposited per exchange, after its request\n    63\tevent, whatever the cause: the peer closed, a write failed, an effect was\n    64\tout of order, the composition's judge reclaimed it, the boundary stopped.\n    65\t`:http/ended` is reserved for a response the boundary completed by\n    66\tperforming an `:http/end` effect. A terminal event reports that the\n    67\texchange ended; it says nothing about which individual effects reached the\n    68\tpeer.\n    69\t\n    70\t## Deposit admission\n    71\t\n    72\tAs `dao.stream.ws.md`, Deposit Admission. The constructor takes the\n    73\tingress destination and its admission declaration as data: evict-oldest\n    74\tretention with a declared capacity, value domain `:portable-values`, able\n    75\tto carry every envelope above, and a lifetime longer than the boundary.\n    76\tWiring a reject-mode destination is a host assembly defect refused at\n    77\tassembly. The constructor also takes `:http/max-open`, the number of\n    78\texchanges held open at once, and `:http/max-body-bytes`; a request beyond\n    79\teither is answered below the transform and never deposited.\n    80\t\n    81\tEviction is loss. A reader whose cursor spans an ingress `gap` has missed\n    82\trequest or terminal events and cannot reconstruct them from this medium;\n    83\tthe inventory above is where the possessing side's truth is.\n    84\t\n    85\t## Effects out\n    86\t\n    87\t`egress-step` is a step the composition drives at its own cadence, as it\n    88\tdrives `endpoint-step`. It holds one cursor on the egress medium, minted at\n    89\t`:dao.stream/newest` before the listener starts, and performs effects in\n    90\tthe egress medium's order:\n    91\t\n    92\t```clojure\n    93\t{:http/exchange [7 42] :http/effect :http/head\n    94\t :http/status 200 :http/headers {\"content-type\" \"text/plain\"}}\n    95\t{:http/exchange [7 42] :http/effect :http/chunk :http/body \"hello\"}\n    96\t{:http/exchange [7 42] :http/effect :http/end}\n    97\t```\n    98\t\n    99\t`:http/head` is sent once and first; `:http/chunk` writes bytes, a string\n   100\tas UTF-8, and flushes; `:http/end` completes the response and the\n   101\texchange. The step appends no per-effect outcome, and a performed write is\n   102\tnot a delivery. A value that is not an effect for an exchange this boundary\n   103\tholds open is not this interpreter's and is ignored, whether it names\n   104\tnothing, names an exchange already ended or closed, or arrives after an\n   105\t`:http/end`: the egress medium has no privileged reader, and other\n   106\tinterpreters may share it. An effect out of order for an open exchange, a\n   107\tchunk before the head or a second head, closes that exchange.\n   108\t\n   109\tOrder is per medium. The ingress and egress media are two sequences, and\n   110\tno ordering holds between them; a writer that must know whether its effect\n   111\twas performed asks a question the wire answers, by what the peer later\n   112\tsends, not this boundary.\n   113\t\n   114\tThe step takes no clock and holds no timer. How long a silent exchange is\n   115\theld open is the composition's, through `dao.lease.md`; `close-exchange` is\n   116\tthe reclaim procedure a judge is composed with.\n   117\t\n   118\t## Observably gone\n   119\t\n   120\tA boundary that cannot deposit or cannot write makes itself observably\n   121\tgone by closing a resource it itself holds, the same action for every\n   122\tcause (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces):\n   123\t\n   124\t- a non-`ok` ingress deposit closes that exchange's socket with no\n   125\t  synthesized response; the terminal event has nowhere to land, which is\n   126\t  the contract's irreducible corner;\n   127\t- a failed socket write closes the exchange and deposits `:http/closed`;\n   128\t- an egress `gap` closes every open exchange, since the step cannot say\n   129\t  whose effects were lost, then adopts the recovery cursor;\n   130\t- an egress `end` or `transport-error` closes every open exchange and the\n   131\t  listener: the boundary can never answer again.\n   132\t\n   133\tIt invokes nothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing.\n   134\t\n   135\t## No privileged node\n   136\t\n   137\tWhich end accepted is an establishment fact: HTTP has a requester and a\n   138\tresponder because TCP has a dialer and an acceptor, and a browser can only\n   139\tdial (`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 1). The ingress and egress media are\n   140\tordinary streams, so a peer that cannot listen may run the answering\n   141\tinterpreter against reflections of them served by a reachable peer that\n   142\truns only this boundary. The boundary records no role beyond the exchange.\n   143\t\n   144\tHosts: clj (http-kit is already a dependency of the WebSocket transport),\n   145\tcljs on Node, cljd. A browser has no listener.\n   146\t\n   147\t## Deferred\n   148\t\n   149\t- An HTTP channel for `dao.stream.remote.md` (request maps by POST, answers\n   150\t  by a long-lived response) for peers that cannot open a WebSocket.\n   151\t- Request bodies as streams rather than one bounded value.\n   152\t- TLS termination, CORS and compression: below the transform, unspecified\n   153\t  here.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
