{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '243,355p'\"","aggregated_output":"   243\t  `next` budget. The chase runs with `(min k chase-budget)`, so one call\n   244\t  costs at most mirror-budget × chase-budget handle operations.\n   245\t\n   246\tA channel writer that answers `:dao.stream/full` on the answer of a\n   247\t`descriptor`, `cursor`, `next` or named `descriptor` request stops the step,\n   248\twhich returns the cursor preceding that request: the request is re-read\n   249\tnext call and the source op recomputes an equally true answer (2.5). A\n   250\t`full` on an `append!` answer does not rewind, since the source append\n   251\talready ran and re-applying it would duplicate; that answer is dropped and\n   252\tthe asker's append stays unknown (2.5). Any other refused write leaves the\n   253\trequest unanswered.\n   254\t\n   255\tSteps 0 to 4 are the mirror's only contacts with the entry's handle: no\n   256\tanswer path calls a handle outside `apply-request`, so nothing bypasses the\n   257\tentry's middleware or its declared surface, and a reflection mints every\n   258\tanchor, `:oldest` and `:newest` included, by request.\n   259\t\n   260\tThe mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or an\n   261\toutcome. The served stream is the original: its cursors, its positions, its\n   262\t`gap`, its `end`. This is the whole basis of the claim in the title, and it\n   263\trests on cursors being plain data that survive the codec (OD-3 decision 2,\n   264\taccepted). A handle whose cursors hold a host object cannot be served, and\n   265\ta composition refuses to enter it into a table.\n   266\t\n   267\t### 2.4 The reflection\n   268\t\n   269\t`attach!` on a remote descriptor returns `:dao.stream/ok` at once with a\n   270\treflection handle and a local, opaque `:dao.stream/attachment`. This is the\n   271\tcontract's deferred remote confirmation (`dao.stream.md`, attach outcomes).\n   272\tThe host-composed attach closure keeps one link per channel descriptor,\n   273\tshared by every reflection through that channel. A link holds: the channel\n   274\twriter; the channel reader and the link's own cursor on it; the set of\n   275\toutstanding request ids with the request each was sent for; filed answers\n   276\tkeyed by id; the `more` outcomes installed at the cursor that precedes\n   277\teach; an optional event writer; and the policy data\n   278\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`, `:dao.stream.remote/budget` and the\n   279\tthree bounds below, each a positive integer or nil for unbounded, any other\n   280\tvalue a composition error at `links`\n   281\t(`ex-info \"invalid DaoStream remote link policy\" {:policy policy}`):\n   282\t\n   283\t- `:dao.stream.remote/drain-budget`: the most channel reads one drain takes.\n   284\t- `:dao.stream.remote/max-outstanding`: the most requests the link holds\n   285\t  outstanding or kept unsent. A send that would pass it is refused locally\n   286\t  as though the writer answered `full`, nothing crossing: `cursor` and\n   287\t  `resolve` answer `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true`, `next`\n   288\t  answers `blocked`, and `append!` answers `{:dao.stream/outcome\n   289\t  :dao.stream/full}`, its effect known. A refused attach probe is kept on\n   290\t  the link only while there is room under the same bound, so outstanding\n   291\t  plus kept never exceeds it and each kept probe's retry, which does not\n   292\t  count itself, always has room. A probe without room waits on its own\n   293\t  reflection, and that reflection's next operation offers it again.\n   294\t- `:dao.stream.remote/max-filed`: the most filed answers plus installed\n   295\t  `more` outcomes the link retains. Past it the oldest retained is evicted,\n   296\t  in the order the link retained them, so filing an answer never evicts\n   297\t  that answer. A prefetch only fills free capacity and never evicts: no\n   298\t  more outcomes are installed than fit beside what is retained, and a\n   299\t  `next` request's stamped budget is at most `max-filed`.\n   300\t  Everything filed is idempotent-recomputable and `append!` answers are\n   301\t  never filed, so the operation an evicted entry answered asks again and\n   302\t  no outcome is invented.\n   303\t\n   304\tOn attach the link sends one `descriptor` request for the identity. Its\n   305\tanswer is the reflection's confirmation. The `not-found` error marks the\n   306\treflection **gone**; `ok` records the source's descriptor and declared\n   307\tsurface. Either is appended to the event writer when one is composed.\n   308\t\n   309\t**Drain.** Every operation on any reflection first reads the link's channel\n   310\treader to `blocked`, or until `drain-budget` reads (`ok` and `gap` alike),\n   311\tfiling each answer whose id is outstanding, installing each\n   312\t`:dao.stream.remote/more` outcome at the cursor that precedes it, and\n   313\tfiling each protocol error under its id. A drain the budget stops keeps\n   314\tthe link's cursor where it stopped: this operation answers from what was\n   315\tfiled so far, and the next operation's drain continues. At most the link's\n   316\town budget `k` less one `more` outcomes are installed per answer, none when\n   317\tthe link stamped no budget; a peer's surplus is dropped. Answers whose id\n   318\tis not outstanding are dropped. Non-answer values are dropped as\n   319\tdiagnostics.\n   320\tThis is where the asynchrony goes (`dao.stream.md`, Where the asynchrony\n   321\tgoes): the caller's own polling is the cadence, and no driver step exists.\n   322\t\n   323\tThen the operation answers as follows.\n   324\t\n   325\t- `descriptor`: local. `ok` with the remote descriptor it was attached with\n   326\t  and `:dao.stream/identity`. Same identity as the source's own descriptor,\n   327\t  different reachability.\n   328\t- `cursor` `a`: a filed answer for `a` is returned, and a filed protocol\n   329\t  error is translated per the rules below. Otherwise a `cursor` request for\n   330\t  `a` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is `transport-error`\n   331\t  with `:dao.stream/retry? true`: the source's map, `invalid-anchor`\n   332\t  included, arrives later as an answer.\n   333\t- `next` `c`: a filed answer for `c` is returned and forgotten. Otherwise a\n   334\t  `next` request for `c` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is\n   335\t  `blocked`: nothing is observable at this position through this handle\n   336\t  yet.\n   337\t- `append!` `v`: send the request and return the channel writer's outcome.\n   338\t  `ok` is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly `dao.stream.md`\n   339\t  Writing's rule for a transport that carries values toward a stream\n   340\t  elsewhere. The source's outcome is appended to the event writer when it\n   341\t  is filed, correlated by id; without an event writer it is dropped.\n   342\t- `close!`: local. The reflection forgets its outstanding ids; afterwards\n   343\t  `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers `closed`, `next` answers\n   344\t  filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.\n   345\t\n   346\t**Protocol errors at the reflection.** A filed `not-found` marks the\n   347\treflection gone; once gone, every later `cursor`, `next` and `append!`\n   348\tanswers `transport-error` with\n   349\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`, while\n   350\t`descriptor` still answers `ok`: a descriptor outlives what it described.\n   351\tA filed\n   352\t`no-surface` or `oversize` marks nothing; each is returned, for the\n   353\toperation that caused it, as `transport-error` with\n   354\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason` set to the error's name. A reflection marked\n   355\tgone is how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6). A request send\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '140,185p;291,315p'\"","aggregated_output":"   140\t### The pass\n   141\t\n   142\tA pass classifies nothing until a first tick has been observed. At each pass,\n   143\tin this order:\n   144\t\n   145\t1. **Drain every wired tick cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`. The newest\n   146\t   reading drained, or the newest previously observed if none were drained, is\n   147\t   **now** for the whole pass.\n   148\t2. **Drain every wired lease-fact cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`, stamping\n   149\t   each fact with that same *now*, and apply it: an eligible renewal advances\n   150\t   the lease's last relevant observation to *now* and returns it to `known`; a\n   151\t   valid `:released` from the holder marks it released; a `:dao.stream/gap`\n   152\t   marks every lease on that medium `unknown` with *now* as its resumed\n   153\t   reading. Each cursor's drain may be bounded by a composition-declared\n   154\t   budget; a cursor still yielding when the budget is spent has not completed\n   155\t   its observation, and while any wired fact cursor is so truncated, the pass\n   156\t   classifies no `:silence` for the leases that truncated medium carries —\n   157\t   `:release`, `:cap`, `:policy` and pending retries are never suppressed. An\n   158\t   incomplete drain may delay a reclaim; it may never manufacture one.\n   159\t3. **Answer and grant.** Proposals drained this pass may be answered, and\n   160\t   unsolicited grants authored, both seeded and stamped with this pass's *now*.\n   161\t   A grant authored between passes is seeded with the next pass's *now*.\n   162\t4. **Classify.** A lease is due for reclaim when any of these holds, and the\n   163\t   first that holds in this order is its cause: it is already `pending`, and\n   164\t   keeps the cause it carries; a valid `:released` from its holder has been\n   165\t   observed, `:release`; its tenure start is further back than\n   166\t   `:dao.lease/max`, `:cap`; the grantor's own policy ends it, `:policy`; its\n   167\t   interval since the last relevant observation exceeds duration plus\n   168\t   tolerance, `:silence`. A lease whose evidence state is `unknown` is due for\n   169\t   `:silence` only once, in addition, a full duration has passed since its\n   170\t   resumed reading.\n   171\t5. **Reclaim** each due lease by its composition-supplied procedure, marking it\n   172\t   `pending` with its cause first. The procedure is idempotent and reports\n   173\t   whether it succeeded; a reclaim that fails or does not report leaves the\n   174\t   lease `pending` and unrecorded.\n   175\t6. **Record** `:lapsed`, carrying the cause the ledger holds, for each lease\n   176\t   whose reclaim succeeded. On `:dao.stream/ok` the lease leaves the ledger;\n   177\t   otherwise it stays `pending` and the next pass reaches it again through the\n   178\t   first clause of step 4.\n   179\t\n   180\tThe record follows the act and never precedes it. **The absence of a `:lapsed`\n   181\tfact is never evidence of tenure.**\n   182\t\n   183\tA drain that ends in `:dao.stream/end` retires that cursor for subsequent\n   184\tpasses. A drain that ends in `:dao.stream/transport-error` ends the pass before\n   185\tstep 4.\n   291\t## Carriage\n   292\t\n   293\tProposals, grants, refusals, releases and renewals cross a boundary as ordinary\n   294\tpayload, carried by the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage\n   295\tis delivery, not a second authoring.\n   296\t\n   297\tA renewal, like a proposal and a release, may be carried by the\n   298\tauthority's front under the holder's request identity, with the same\n   299\tresolver attribution and the same carriage as the proposal and\n   300\trelease it accompanies (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2.4). The judge\n   301\tcounts the carried renewal; carriage adds no key to any outcome map.\n   302\t\n   303\t`:lapsed` does not cross; it is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream. A\n   304\tremote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a served connection, an\n   305\tordinary `:ws/closed`; for a stream served under `dao.stream.remote.md`, the\n   306\t`not-found` protocol error its mirror answers for the reclaimed identity. A remote holder that\n   307\treads the grantor's stream through a served handle is observing the grantor's\n   308\trecord, not receiving carriage. No close code is assigned here; distinguishing\n   309\treclaim on the wire belongs to `dao.stream.ws.md`'s deferred close-code design.\n   310\t\n   311\t## Out of scope\n   312\t\n   313\t- **Delegated renewal.** A renewal is evidence about its author.\n   314\t- **Transfer of a lease between holders.**\n   315\t- **Who may be granted anything at all**, and **what is leasable** — this\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
